(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-3-fungi-of-medical-importance-quiz"]={"title":"Fungi of Medical Importance — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Where does Histoplasma capsulatum grow in nature?","choices":["Cool, humid soils of South America","Decaying plants, pine straw and hay","Moist, nitrogen-rich soil with guano","Alkaline, salty soil of hot, dry land"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Histoplasma capsulatum grows in moist soil with a high nitrogen content, especially soil containing bird and bat guano. When wind or digging disturbs that soil, the conidia become airborne and are inhaled. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 26, 30, 37 and 39 and image on Slide 28"},{"q":"To what practice can the resistance of Aspergillus to azole fungicides be attributed?","choices":["Long topical courses for ringworm","Amphotericin B in transplant care","Agricultural crop spraying","Hospital disinfectant overuse"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aspergillus is a very common airborne soil fungus, and its resistance to azole fungicides can be attributed to agricultural practices: spraying crops to prevent losses due to fungi. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 19, 20, 45, 52 and 60"},{"q":"How is tinea pedis mainly spread?","choices":["Puncture by thorns or splinters","Going barefoot as a daily habit","Exposure to public surfaces","Contact with infected animals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tinea pedis (foot) and tinea manuum (hand) are spread by exposure to public surfaces and occur between the digits and on the soles. Tellingly, tinea pedis is rare where people usually go barefoot, so it is the shared surfaces, not bare feet, that matter. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 39, 43, 44 and 45"},{"q":"Which traits make hospital outbreaks of Candida auris hard to eradicate?","choices":["Switching between mold and yeast forms","Drug, heat and disinfectant resistance","A capsule shown by negative stain","Arthroconidia spread by windstorms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Candida auris is emerging as a serious cause of invasive candidiasis, with hospital outbreaks that are difficult to eradicate: it shows antifungal drug resistance, is thermoresistant and is partially resistant to common disinfectants. It was first identified in an ear infection, hence its name. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 5, 14, 30, 52, 56 and 57"},{"q":"A student who shares bedding develops inflamed, red ring-shaped lesions on the smooth skin of the trunk. Which name fits this ringworm?","choices":["Tinea capitis","Tinea unguium","Tinea corporis","Tinea cruris"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tinea corporis, ringworm of the body, occurs as inflamed, red ring lesions anywhere on smooth skin and spreads by contact, clothing and bedding. Rings on smooth trunk skin after shared bedding are the decisive clues. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 44 and 45"},{"q":"A yeast isolate incubated in serum for 2 to 4 hours grows short hyphal sprouts. Which identification test is this?","choices":["Hair infection test","Test for dimorphism","Periodic acid-Schiff stain","Germ tube test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Some yeasts, when incubated in serum for 2 to 4 hours, sprout tiny hyphal tubes called germ tubes. The germ tube test sits among the identification tests for isolates from blood, cerebrospinal fluid, pus and vaginal secretions, alongside differential media and biochemical and antigen tests. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, image on Slide 17"},{"q":"Into what form do inhaled Blastomyces conidia convert in the lungs?","choices":["Spherules full of endospores","Branched septate hyphae","Block-like arthroconidia","Multiplying yeast cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blastomyces is a dimorphic fungus, free-living in soil; its inhaled conidia convert to yeasts that multiply in the lungs, the yeast phase that dimorphic fungi adopt at body temperature in an animal host. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 6, 18, 30 and 33 and image on Slide 34"},{"q":"Why is immunization typically ineffective for preventing fungal infections?","choices":["Reinfection is exceedingly common","Fungi cause too few human infections","Fungi turn into yeasts inside the host","Antifungal drugs already prevent infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immunization is typically ineffective against fungal infections because reinfection is exceedingly common. Control therefore relies on antifungal drugs (amphotericin B, flucytosine, azoles and nystatin), surgical removal of damaged tissue where needed, and limiting contact with spores. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 4, 5, 7, 12 and 19"},{"q":"Blastomyces dermatitidis lies dormant in warm, dry weather. When does it sporulate?","choices":["In colder, wetter weather","In hot, dry summer weather","After drought, rain and windstorms","Inside the host at body heat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blastomyces is dormant in warm, dry weather and sporulates in the colder, wetter weather that follows, releasing the conidia that people inhale. This cycle plays out in the soils of the midwestern and southeastern United States and southern Canada. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 30 and 33 and image on Slide 6"},{"q":"Sporothrix schenckii is a saprobic fungus. What does saprobic mean?","choices":["It feeds on dead or decaying matter","It must live on a living host","It switches from mold to yeast at 37 C","It lives as normal flora on human skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A saprobe derives its nutrition from dead or decaying matter. Sporothrix schenckii is a very common saprobic fungus that decomposes plant matter in soil, which is why thorns, wood, bare roots, pine straw and hay carry it to people. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 5, 12, 39 and 53"},{"q":"How do mycotoxicoses usually arise?","choices":["Fungi invading through broken skin","Spores provoking a hypersensitivity","Overgrowth of resident yeast flora","Ingesting or inhaling fungal toxins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mycotoxicoses are usually caused by ingesting or inhaling fungal toxins. Examples are aflatoxin on grains, corn and peanuts and ergot on rye or other grains contaminated by Claviceps fungi; they are intoxications, not infections. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 38, 52 and 65"},{"q":"Which community measure reduces inhalation of Coccidioides spores in an endemic area?","choices":["Oiling roads to keep down dust","Vaccinating outdoor workers","Isolating Valley fever patients","Clearing pigeon roosts from buildings"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Coccidioides arthrospores are inhaled from dust, so prevention targets dust: oiling roads, planting to minimize erosion and using dust masks. Farming, road construction or repair, archaeological digs, landslides and dust storms all raise exposure. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 12, 19, 26, 30 and 56"},{"q":"Which fungi naturally inhabit the human body and can pass person to person or by fomites such as shared exercise equipment?","choices":["Dermatophytes and Candida","Histoplasma and Blastomyces","Coccidioides and Paracoccidioides","Aspergillus and Cryptococcus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Unlike most fungal pathogens, which need no host and are generally not communicable, dermatophytes (literally 'skin plants') and Candida naturally inhabit the human body and are transmissible from person to person or by fomites such as shared exercise equipment. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 12, 26, 30, 33, 37, 56 and 60"},{"q":"On what basis do most scientists now classify Pneumocystis as a fungus rather than a protozoan?","choices":["Its antibody titers","Its genetic analysis","Its response to antifungals","Its growth as hyphae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The taxonomy of Pneumocystis is still debated, fungus or protozoan, but most scientists lean toward a fungal classification because of genetic analysis. Its usual drugs do not settle the question: one is an antiprotozoal and the other an antibacterial. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 58 and image on Slide 59"},{"q":"A negative stain of a specimen shows encapsulated budding yeast. Which organism does this demonstrate?","choices":["Candida albicans","Aspergillus fumigatus","Pneumocystis jirovecii","Cryptococcus neoformans"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A negative stain demonstrating encapsulated budding yeast is the picture of Cryptococcus neoformans, the widespread encapsulated yeast of pigeon-roost soil. Biochemical and serological testing are the best diagnostic tools for it. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 17, 18, 55, 56, 57 and 58 and image on Slide 59"},{"q":"A fungal infection involves the stratum corneum and occasionally the upper dermis, but no deeper. What level of invasion is this?","choices":["Superficial","Subcutaneous","Systemic","Cutaneous"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous involvement takes in the stratum corneum and occasionally the upper dermis. It is deeper than the extremely shallow epidermal colonization of superficial infection but stops short of the subcutaneous tissue reached after a puncture wound. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 14 and image on Slide 23"},{"q":"How can Fusarium reach and infect the eye?","choices":["Spread from the nose in diabetes","Eating grain contaminated by it","Through contaminated contact lenses","From close person-to-person contact"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fusarium species, a mainly tropical soil mold, occasionally infect the eyes, for example through contaminated contact lenses, as well as toenails, feet and burned skin, and can result in severe disability and death. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 12, 63, 64 and 65"},{"q":"Antibiotic therapy and catheters predispose patients to which opportunistic fungus?","choices":["Fusarium species","Candida species","Aspergillus species","Cryptococcus species"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Among the common opportunistic fungi, Candida is the one associated with antibiotic therapy and catheters, alongside diabetes, corticosteroids and immunosuppression. It is the dominant opportunistic pathogen and its infections are usually endogenous. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 51, 52 and 64 and image on Slide 9"},{"q":"At which temperature does a thermally dimorphic fungus grow as unicellular yeast?","choices":["20 C","25 C","30 C","37 C"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thermally dimorphic fungi grow as multicellular molds at 30 C and as unicellular yeasts at 37 C, human body temperature; in the animal habitat (35 to 40 C) they live as parasitic, budding yeasts. Culturing at room temperature and at 37 C demonstrates the switch. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 5 and 17 and image on Slide 6"},{"q":"Which factor may lead to white or black piedra?","choices":["Poor hygiene, such as oily hair products","Close contact with an infected person","Handling infected household animals","Inhaling spores from bird guano"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both piedras are considered non-contagious and may result from poor personal hygiene, such as oily hair products and tight hats or veils. They are superficial mycoses of the hair shaft, and neither is common in the United States. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 12, 26, 44, 49 and 50"},{"q":"On the World Health Organization's fungal priority list, which fungus is ranked one tier above Coccidioides?","choices":["Histoplasma species","Paracoccidioides species","Pneumocystis jirovecii","Cryptococcus gattii"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Coccidioides is in the medium priority group; Histoplasma species are in the high priority group, one tier above, with other Candida species, Fusarium, the eumycetoma agents and the Mucorales. The critical group sits above both. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 11, 56 and 58"},{"q":"Which measure is the main way to prevent fungal infection by reducing contact with fungal spores?","choices":["Routine antifungal vaccination","Preventive amphotericin B","Isolating infected patients","High-quality respirator masks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Methods of prevention are essentially limited to wearing high-quality respirator masks and protective clothing, with hygiene, to reduce contact with fungal spores; the primary mycoses are caught by inhaling spores from the air. Immunization is typically ineffective. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 12, 14, 19 and 20"},{"q":"Which treatment is used for systemic sporotrichosis?","choices":["Pentamidine and/or co-trimoxazole","Topical azoles and debridement","Amphotericin B and/or itraconazole","Heat packs and/or potassium iodide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Systemic sporotrichosis is treated with amphotericin B and/or itraconazole. Heat packs and/or super-saturated potassium iodide are the treatment given for infection of the appendages and lungs; once the disease is systemic, the drug pair is used. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 39, 45 and 58"},{"q":"Which description fits paracoccidioidomycosis?","choices":["An opportunist needing impaired defenses","Rarest primary mycosis, usually mild","Commonest primary mycosis, usually mild","Rarest primary mycosis, usually systemic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Paracoccidioidomycosis, endemic in Central and South America (mainly the cool, humid soils of Brazil, Colombia, Venezuela, Argentina and Paraguay), is the rarest of the primary mycoses; systemic disease is not common, and infections are usually mild and self-limiting. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 5, 7, 26 and 37 and image on Slide 13"},{"q":"Which ability defines a true (primary) fungal pathogen?","choices":["Staying a mold at human body temperature","Invading and growing in a healthy host","Needing impaired host defenses to infect","Spreading readily from person to person"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A true or primary fungal pathogen can invade and grow in a healthy host. Its most striking adaptation is the switch from hyphal cells to yeast cells with the change to host temperature. An opportunist, by contrast, has little or no virulence of its own and needs impaired host defenses. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 5, 7 and 12"},{"q":"A wrestler living in a crowded dormitory has ringworm of the groin and scrotal skin. Which name fits?","choices":["Tinea cruris","Tinea pedis","Tinea barbae","Tinea manuum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tinea cruris, the jock itch, affects the groin and scrotal regions and is pervasive among athletes and in crowded living conditions. An athlete in crowded housing with groin and scrotal involvement fits it exactly. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 44 and 45"},{"q":"A dye-stained sputum specimen shows a mass of branched septate hyphae and no yeast cells. Which fungus does this fit?","choices":["Coccidioides immitis","Aspergillus species","Candida albicans","Cryptococcus neoformans"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Branched septate hyphae in a sputum specimen are the picture of Aspergillus, an airborne soil fungus whose spores germinate in the lungs and can form fungal balls. Dyes amplify these fungal elements in tissue specimens. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 30, 55, 57 and 60 and image on Slide 18"},{"q":"How does Pneumocystis pneumonia become rapidly fatal when it is not controlled?","choices":["Spherules with endospores form nodules","Spread to the meninges and brain","Secretions in the lungs block breathing","Fungal balls form in the lungs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pneumocystis pneumonia forms secretions in the lungs that block breathing; with cough, fever, shallow respiration and cyanosis, it can be rapidly fatal if not controlled with medication. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 30, 56, 58 and 60"},{"q":"In an immunocompromised patient, where does Cryptococcus neoformans spread after the lungs, with the gravest results?","choices":["The nail beds of hands and feet","The meninges and brain","Nearby lymph nodes","The sinuses and ear canals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lung infection by Cryptococcus neoformans brings cough, fever and lung nodules; dissemination to the meninges and the brain can cause severe neurological disturbance and death in compromised hosts. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 39, 45, 56 and 60"},{"q":"A fungus with few, if any, virulence factors causes a systemic infection in a patient taking long-term corticosteroids. How is this mycosis best classified?","choices":["Dermatophytosis","Opportunistic mycosis","True (primary) mycosis","Subcutaneous mycosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Few, if any, virulence factors plus a host weakened by corticosteroids is the picture of an opportunistic mycosis: these fungi usually start infection in immunocompromised hosts, and predisposing factors such as primary illness, corticosteroid use, diabetes and cancer should be considered. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 5, 7, 14, 22 and 42"}]};
